SUMMARY:WIP presentations — Emory Decision-Making Megalab
DESCRIPTION:Works in progress. Works-in-progress presentations from Pete 
 Hitchcock on a decision-science approach to negative self-judgment in de
 pression and Michael Hallquist on learning what to choose and when to ac
 t\, connecting response vigor\, matching\, and free-operant behavior\, w
 ith time for discussion and feedback. Background papers for both present
 ations are linked with this meeting. No advance reading is required. Pre
 sentations: Pete Hitchcock: Making Progress on Inherently Complex ("Low 
 Essentiality") Disorders: A Case Study. Hitchcock introduces the idea th
 at mental health problems vary in their inherent complexity and argues t
 hat depression and generalized anxiety are inherently complex\, or low i
 n essentiality. As a case study in making progress on these problems\, h
 e describes the lab’s decision-science approach to negative self-judgmen
 t in depression. This work builds on sequential-sampling models\, in whi
 ch decisions unfold by accumulating evidence over time: visual evidence 
 for perceptual judgments\, such as identifying a traffic light’s color\,
  and personal value associations for food choices\, such as choosing bet
 ween Butterfingers and Reese’s.\; Michael Hallquist: Learning when to ac
 t and what to choose. Hallquist explores how learning shapes both action
  choice and response timing\, connecting response vigor with the matchin
 g law. He compares computational models across reward schedules\, extinc
 tion\, changing reward environments\, and tasks that reward waiting. The
  talk highlights where the models succeed and fall short\, and proposes 
 human studies to test their potential relevance to anhedonia\, effort-re
 lated inactivity\, and impulsive action.. Optional background: Decision 
 Making and Sequential Sampling from Memory. Authors: Shadlen & Shohamy. 
 Journal: Neuron (2016). Shared by Pete Hitchcock: proposes that value-ba
 sed choices accumulate evidence retrieved from memory\, extending sequen
 tial-sampling accounts of perceptual decisions. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih
 .gov/articles/PMC4891701/ Optional background: The hippocampus supports 
 deliberation during value-based decisions. Authors: Bakkour\, Palombo\, 
 Zylberberg\, Kang\, Reid\, Verfaellie\, Shadlen & Shohamy. Journal: eLif
 e (2019). Shared by Pete Hitchcock: fMRI and evidence from patients with
  hippocampal damage support a role for the hippocampus in deliberation d
 uring value-based choices. https://elifesciences.org/articles/46080 Back
 ground reading: Tonic dopamine: Opportunity costs and the control of res
 ponse vigor. Authors: Niv\, Daw\, Joel & Dayan. Journal: Psychopharmacol
 ogy (2007). Background on average reward rate\, the opportunity cost of 
 time\, and response vigor. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17031711/ Bac
 kground reading: On two types of deviation from the matching law: Bias a
 nd undermatching. Authors: Baum. Journal: Journal of the Experimental An
 alysis of Behavior (1974). Background on how behavior is allocated acros
 s competing sources of reinforcement. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/artic
 les/PMC1333261/ Optional background: A comparison of variable-ratio and 
 variable-interval schedules of reinforcement. Authors: Zuriff. Journal: 
 Journal of the Experimental Analysis of Behavior (1970). Shows how diffe
 rent reinforcement contingencies can produce different response rates ev
 en at equal reinforcement rates. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/P
 MC1333678/ Lead: Pete Hitchcock and Michael Hallquist. Location: PAIS 28
 0.
